    "review::.::holistic::low_level_elegance::storage-status-conversion-duplication::949ad5e8": {
      "id": "review::.::holistic::low_level_elegance::storage-status-conversion-duplication::949ad5e8",
      "detector": "review",
      "file": ".",
      "tier": 3,
      "confidence": "high",
      "summary": "persisted_status_to_registry_status and turso_status_to_registry_status are structurally identical functions with trivial differences",
      "detail": {
        "holistic": true,
        "dimension": "low_level_elegance",
        "related_files": [
          "crates/temper-cli/src/serve/storage.rs"
        ],
        "evidence": [
          "persisted_status_to_registry_status (lines 130-176) and turso_status_to_registry_status (lines 338-384) have identical control flow, identical match arms (pending/running/fallback), and identical level-reconstruction logic. The only material difference is the input row type (PersistedSpecRow vs TursoSpecRow) and the verification_result field type (serde_json::Value vs Option<String>). Similarly, load_registry_from_postgres and load_registry_from_turso (lines 178-335) duplicate the entire grouping-by-tenant, CSDL parsing, and registration logic."
        ],
        "suggestion": "Define a trait (e.g. SpecRowLike) with accessors for the common fields, then implement it for both row types. Write a single generic load_registry_from_rows function and a single status_to_registry_status function parameterized over the trait.",
        "reasoning": ""
      },
      "status": "auto_resolved",
      "note": "Disappeared from scan \u2014 likely fixed",
      "first_seen": "2026-03-05T17:18:48+00:00",
