    "review::.::holistic::error_consistency::mixed-error-mapping-patterns::7ea3858d": {
      "id": "review::.::holistic::error_consistency::mixed-error-mapping-patterns::7ea3858d",
      "detector": "review",
      "file": ".",
      "tier": 3,
      "confidence": "medium",
      "summary": "Inconsistent error mapping: some code uses .context() (anyhow), others use .map_err(|e| anyhow::anyhow!(...)), and HTTP handlers use bare String errors",
      "detail": {
        "holistic": true,
        "dimension": "error_consistency",
        "related_files": [
          "crates/temper-cli/src/serve/storage.rs",
          "crates/temper-sandbox/src/http.rs",
          "crates/temper-server/src/state/dispatch/actions.rs"
        ],
        "evidence": [
          "In storage.rs, connect_postgres_store uses .context() (line 45) alongside .map_err(|e| anyhow::anyhow!(...)) (lines 48, 53) for the same function's error handling. In http.rs, temper_request returns Result<Value, String> (line 21) using format!() for errors. In dispatch/actions.rs, dispatch_tenant_action returns Result<EntityResponse, String>. The codebase has three distinct error strategies: anyhow in CLI, String in server dispatch/HTTP, and thiserror in library crates (McpError, ObserveError)."
        ],
        "suggestion": "The layered error strategy (thiserror in libraries, anyhow in CLI) is reasonable, but the String error returns in temper-server dispatch functions lose type information. Consider a ServerDispatchError enum for the dispatch layer, converting to String only at the HTTP boundary.",
        "reasoning": ""
      },
