    "review::.::holistic::design_coherence::evolution_persistence_split_brain::653eb7aa": {
      "id": "review::.::holistic::design_coherence::evolution_persistence_split_brain::653eb7aa",
      "detector": "review",
      "file": ".",
      "tier": 3,
      "confidence": "medium",
      "summary": "Evolution record typing and persistence mapping is split across layers with duplicated string conventions.",
      "detail": {
        "holistic": true,
        "dimension": "design_coherence",
        "related_files": [
          "crates/temper-evolution/src/pg_store.rs",
          "crates/temper-server/src/observe/evolution/operations.rs"
        ],
        "evidence": [
          "`PostgresRecordStore` exposes many typed wrapper methods over generic `(record_type,status,payload)` storage, but `operations.rs` separately constructs record-type/status strings (`\"Observation\"`, `format!(\"{:?}\", status)`) and writes through a different store path.",
          "This duplicates domain mapping logic (enum <-> string) across server and store layers, increasing drift risk when record types/status values evolve."
        ],
        "suggestion": "Move record-type/status serialization and persistence orchestration behind one evolution persistence facade that accepts typed records; let HTTP handlers call typed operations only, and keep string mapping confined to the store boundary.",
        "reasoning": ""
